WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.460
The billing specifics in the preview

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:03.580
are fascinating and require extreme

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:06.619
caution. Currently what Microsoft categorizes

00:00:06.619 --> 00:00:08.380
as the discovery and AI reasoning

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:10.520
phases, the part where the LLM

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:11.960
reads your prompt and figures out

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:14.399
what you want, are non -billable

00:00:14.399 --> 00:00:15.499
during the preview phase.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:17.380
No, that sounds great.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:18.920
Free AI reasoning.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:21.120
But, and this is a massive

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.300
trap for the unwary, the child

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.360
operations that the AI triggers and

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.439
the underlying OneLake storage compute

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:29.420
it utilizes to formulate those answers

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:31.840
can and will incur standard Fabric

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.720
capacity charges. Walk me through how

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:35.900
that traps springs.

00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:38.400
Imagine you have a non -technical

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.160
manager who asks the chat interface

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.440
a slightly vague massive question about

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.080
five years of historical inventory

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.320
across the entire globe.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.800
The AI reasoning to understand the

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:48.879
question is free.

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:50.500
But to answer it, the AI

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:52.480
might quietly write a highly inefficient

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:54.300
brute force SQL query that scans

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.400
petabytes of data across your OneLake.

00:00:56.900 --> 00:00:58.300
It pushes that massive compute job

00:00:58.300 --> 00:00:59.139
down to the warehouse.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:00.779
And you pay for that compute.

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:02.540
Suddenly, you aren't paying for the

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.160
chat interaction. You are paying for

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:05.760
an absolutely massive compute spike

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.420
on your Fabric capacity bill, because

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:08.960
the AI decided to brute force

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.380
the answer. The source strongly recommends

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.260
using the Fabric capacity metrics app

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:14.780
to monitor this underlying compute

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.560
consumption closely during any pilot

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:16.954
program.
